Allison Jones as a casting associate?

788
00:32:07.540 --> 00:32:11.960
I mean, Jenna and I were so curious because we realized we know how she got her role in

789
00:32:11.960 --> 00:32:14.700
the office, but we don't know how she ended up in Los Angeles.

790
00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:16.880
Or how she ended up working with Allison.

791
00:32:17.120 --> 00:32:17.740
That's right.

792
00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:22.180
Well, Phyllis sent us in a clip answering your question, Grace, and here it is.

793
00:32:22.180 --> 00:32:27.120
Well, I was on the road in a vaudeville burlesque show.

794
00:32:27.960 --> 00:32:35.400
I was doing like show dancing, the Charleston, the original can-can from the Moulin Rouge.

795
00:32:35.860 --> 00:32:37.540
We did not strip.

796
00:32:37.820 --> 00:32:40.480
We had strategically placed feathers.

797
00:32:40.980 --> 00:32:42.580
It was a lot of fun.

798
00:32:42.580 --> 00:32:47.060
And then I worked with Will Be Able and his show called Baggy Pants.

799
00:32:47.260 --> 00:32:54.100
And then I continued in another vaudeville show working with the Mercer brothers, Bud and

800
00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:54.920
Jim Mercer.

801
00:32:55.300 --> 00:33:02.830
And combined with Baggy Pants and Bud and Jim's show called Giggles Galore, I was on the road

802
00:33:02.830 --> 00:33:04.290
for about six or seven years.

803
00:33:05.150 --> 00:33:09.770
And then the Mercer brothers and I, we decided to take the show to California

804
00:33:10.610 --> 00:33:12.490
to revamp the show.

805
00:33:12.930 --> 00:33:18.910
And the Mercer brothers lived in California, so that's why we decided to take the show to California.

806
00:33:19.290 --> 00:33:24.150
And while we were in the throes of revamping the show, we ended up getting a job

807
00:33:24.150 --> 00:33:26.930
in Long Beach at Shoreline Village.

808
00:33:27.370 --> 00:33:30.550
And the show was called The Captain's Crew Review.

809
00:33:30.970 --> 00:33:36.170
I think the sign is still up there on the boardwalk, if you ever get down that way.

810
00:33:36.610 --> 00:33:40.790
And we did three shows on Saturday, three on Sunday.

811
00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:43.870
And we did that for an entire season.

812
00:33:44.410 --> 00:33:50.150
Then I injured my knee doing a jump split at the end of the can-can.

813
00:33:50.270 --> 00:33:53.310
And so that kind of ended my dancing career.

814
00:33:53.310 --> 00:33:58.270
I ended up becoming a receptionist at an aerospace defense company.

815
00:33:58.570 --> 00:34:04.790
And while I was working there, I got a call to actually go on an audition for a mousy woman.

816
00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:06.810
I went in.

817
00:34:06.970 --> 00:34:10.130
I did not get the role because I wasn't mousy enough.

818
00:34:10.310 --> 00:34:16.870
But during that audition, I told the casting director, I think I might be good in casting.

819
00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:26.690
And then a little while later, I got a call to be her casting coordinator at the Edwards Billet Production Company.

820
00:34:26.930 --> 00:34:31.530
They did things like the court shows, like Superior Court and other shows.

821
00:34:31.870 --> 00:34:35.310
And I met a gentleman by the name of Alan Hochberg.

822
00:34:35.490 --> 00:34:40.470
And we were both working in the casting department at Edwards Billet Productions.

823
00:34:40.470 --> 00:34:46.390
And Alan ended up working as the casting director on Dr. Quinn Medicine Woman.

824
00:34:46.390 --> 00:34:50.490
And he asked me to be his associate with him on Dr. Quinn.

825
00:34:51.150 --> 00:34:54.630
We did Dr. Quinn for the run of the show.

826
00:34:55.090 --> 00:34:59.330
And when it ended, I walked out of the casting office.

827
00:34:59.530 --> 00:35:03.610
And I walked across the hall to another casting director's office.

828
00:35:03.850 --> 00:35:06.790
And I said, hey, guys, see y'all later.

829
00:35:06.970 --> 00:35:10.830
This is my last day with Alan Hochberg.

830
00:35:10.830 --> 00:35:15.410
And if you know of anyone that needs a casting associate, I'm available.

831
00:35:15.810 --> 00:35:20.630
And they said, you know, Alison Jones needs an associate.

832
00:35:21.670 --> 00:35:24.290
So they gave me her number and I called her.

833
00:35:24.590 --> 00:35:25.810
I had an interview with her.

834
00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:28.730
And I started work that following Monday.

835
00:35:30.230 --> 00:35:33.190
And that's how I ended up at Alison Jones.

836
00:35:33.470 --> 00:35:34.990
So it was kind of a meant to be.

837
00:35:35.350 --> 00:35:40.010
I went out the door of one casting office and into another casting office.

838
00:35:40.970 --> 00:35:43.990
And so that's how I came to California.

839
00:35:45.850 --> 00:35:47.650
I mean, a jump split.

840
00:35:48.270 --> 00:35:50.550
That sounds very difficult.

841
00:35:50.550 --> 00:35:52.710
You do jump splits on the trampoline.

842
00:35:52.930 --> 00:35:55.390
I mean, if I don't land in the splits, though, right?

843
00:35:55.790 --> 00:35:56.490
Oh, yes.

844
00:35:56.630 --> 00:35:56.910
I do.

845
00:35:57.390 --> 00:35:57.550
Right.

846
00:35:57.770 --> 00:35:59.150
Yeah, like a toe touch.

847
00:35:59.270 --> 00:36:00.230
But I'm not landing.

848
00:36:00.570 --> 00:36:01.050
Yeah.

849
00:36:01.230 --> 00:36:02.210
I land on my feet.

850
00:36:02.870 --> 00:36:07.170
Well, that just goes to show you that one door closes, another one opens.

851
00:36:07.470 --> 00:36:11.450
And Phyllis walked through it and ended up walking her way onto our show.

852
00:36:12.110 --> 00:36:12.350
Right.

853
00:36:12.710 --> 00:36:17.810
I do just love her sort of just like, get out there, put yourself out there, just walked

854
00:36:17.810 --> 00:36:20.330
across the hall, said, hey, I'm available for a job.

855
00:36:21.050 --> 00:36:23.790
Phyllis talked a little bit about her dancing in that audio clip.

856
00:36:24.030 --> 00:36:27.330
And we got this fan question from Marina M.

857
00:36:27.530 --> 00:36:29.190
in Melbourne, Australia, who said,

858
00:36:29.330 --> 00:36:33.230
I love watching Phyllis dance and the joy she has in her movement.

859
00:36:33.530 --> 00:36:37.110
Could Phyllis give us a little rundown of her dancing background?

860
00:36:37.850 --> 00:36:42.330
Well, Marina, Phyllis told us that she started dancing at seven years old.

861
00:36:42.650 --> 00:36:47.030
And the majority of her training was in St. Louis with Marjorie Mendelia.

862
00:36:47.290 --> 00:36:50.110
Was Marjorie sort of a big deal in St. Louis for dancing?

863
00:36:50.430 --> 00:36:51.930
I don't know, Marjorie.

864
00:36:52.110 --> 00:36:54.270
I studied at Kripinski's.

865
00:36:54.490 --> 00:36:54.730
Oh.

866
00:36:54.730 --> 00:37:00.250
And it has been passed down through three generations now, from Lillian Kripinski to

867
00:37:00.250 --> 00:37:02.390
Mark Kripinski to his daughter.

868
00:37:02.390 --> 00:37:03.630
I don't know her name.

869
00:37:03.790 --> 00:37:07.970
She was a little girl when I was dancing because I studied mostly under Mark.

870
00:37:08.390 --> 00:37:10.250
But I started with Lillian.

871
00:37:10.450 --> 00:37:12.930
Now this is becoming about my dance background, everyone.

872
00:37:13.250 --> 00:37:13.930
Sorry for that.

873
00:37:13.930 --> 00:37:20.010
But Lillian Kripinski, for ballet class, she used to walk around with a cane and she would

874
00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:25.070
whap the floor right next to us, like if our toes weren't pointed correctly or if our

875
00:37:25.070 --> 00:37:26.710
turnout wasn't correct.

876
00:37:26.970 --> 00:37:27.170
Yeah.

877
00:37:27.310 --> 00:37:28.250
Oh, I'd be out.

878
00:37:28.450 --> 00:37:30.690
Well, she retired and Mark took over.

879
00:37:30.850 --> 00:37:33.230
But yeah, so I studied at Kripinski's.

880
00:37:33.370 --> 00:37:38.190
Well, I'm just sort of getting a vibe here from my St. Louis gals that dance has kind of

881
00:37:38.190 --> 00:37:39.610
got a real community there.

882
00:37:40.010 --> 00:37:42.550
I think there is a big dance community in St. Louis.

883
00:37:43.290 --> 00:37:45.450
Okay, so back to Phyllis.

884
00:37:45.650 --> 00:37:48.510
Yes, she said she took ballet, tap, and jazz.

885
00:37:48.750 --> 00:37:54.830
She also danced in two ballet companies, the St. Louis Civic Ballet and the St. Louis Dance

886
00:37:54.830 --> 00:37:55.230
Theater.

887
00:37:55.470 --> 00:37:55.890
Wow.

888
00:37:56.390 --> 00:38:02.070
She did point and she also studied at Kansas City University and Tulsa University in the

889
00:38:02.070 --> 00:38:02.370
summer.

890
00:38:02.650 --> 00:38:07.910
She said they had these six-week intensified workshops that she did for like three or four

891
00:38:07.910 --> 00:38:08.710
different summers.

892
00:38:08.710 --> 00:38:10.810
So a lot of dance training.

893
00:38:10.810 --> 00:38:16.750
This is when I wish we could just take a minute and just like time travel and meet Phyllis back

894
00:38:16.750 --> 00:38:18.590
then and like see her on stage.

895
00:38:18.750 --> 00:38:19.850
Wouldn't that be so fun?

896
00:38:20.010 --> 00:38:20.210
Yeah.

897
00:38:20.870 --> 00:38:25.550
Well, Phyllis also said she ended up working at JCPenney's and she would teach dancing at

898
00:38:25.550 --> 00:38:27.110
night and take lessons at night.

899
00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:31.550
And then she ended up eventually going to Washington University to get her certificate

900
00:38:31.550 --> 00:38:32.990
to teach modern dance.

901
00:38:33.110 --> 00:38:34.310
I didn't know any of this.

902
00:38:34.430 --> 00:38:34.830
Me either.

903
00:38:34.830 --> 00:38:37.810
Unfortunately, she didn't end up getting her certificate.

904
00:38:37.810 --> 00:38:39.850
I guess she missed it by one or two hours.

905
00:38:40.190 --> 00:38:42.610
But she took a ton of dance classes.

906
00:38:42.850 --> 00:38:47.930
And as Phyllis shared with us, she also ended up dancing in a jazz company in San Diego one

907
00:38:47.930 --> 00:38:48.230
summer.

908
00:38:48.450 --> 00:38:51.590
And then that got her into the vaudeville burlesque show scene.

909
00:38:51.710 --> 00:38:56.290
And she was on the road for about six or seven years doing that show.

910
00:38:56.290 --> 00:38:58.310
I guess until the jump split.

911
00:38:58.730 --> 00:39:01.430
I mean, yeah, that is a long time.

912
00:39:01.590 --> 00:39:06.150
And Phyllis said, you know, she always knew she wanted to be a dancer.

913
00:39:06.510 --> 00:39:07.310
I love that.

914
00:39:07.430 --> 00:39:08.450
That was her first love.

915
00:39:08.570 --> 00:39:09.150
Her first love.

916
00:39:09.230 --> 00:39:11.170
And she did get to shimmy and dance on The Office.

917
00:39:11.470 --> 00:39:15.890
Well, it shows that she always wanted to be a dancer because it brought out a joy in

918
00:39:15.890 --> 00:39:17.090
her every time she did it.

919
00:39:17.610 --> 00:39:18.490
Kickball change.

920
00:39:19.130 --> 00:39:20.310
Kickball change, lady.

921
00:39:23.350 --> 00:39:24.310
Pot-a-boo-ray.

922
00:39:25.770 --> 00:39:31.610
Okay, so now that we got the Phyllis Smith backstory, a lot of you wrote in asking if Phyllis

923
00:39:31.610 --> 00:39:34.550
created a backstory for the character of Phyllis.

924
00:39:34.790 --> 00:39:37.630
And if so, could we share some of the details?

925
00:39:38.450 --> 00:39:41.030
Well, we asked Phyllis, and here's what she said.

926
00:39:41.230 --> 00:39:46.750
She said, to be very honest, I was so new as an actor that I generally concentrated on

927
00:39:46.750 --> 00:39:50.670
trying to do the words of the script to be as authentic as I could be.

928
00:39:50.890 --> 00:39:53.670
I really didn't know to create a backstory.

929
00:39:54.310 --> 00:39:58.810
So I think my character developed by how the writers saw my personality.

930
00:39:59.370 --> 00:40:04.470
We had multiple writers on the show, and various writers would see Phyllis different ways.

931
00:40:05.730 --> 00:40:08.070
Phyllis said some of the writers would see her as shy.

932
00:40:08.270 --> 00:40:09.450
Others saw her as sassy.

933
00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:13.010
So she basically just concentrated on the written word.

934
00:40:13.170 --> 00:40:16.690
And that's what brought authenticity to her performance.

935
00:40:16.690 --> 00:40:17.950
I love that.

936
00:40:18.050 --> 00:40:22.410
It sort of reminds me when we spoke to Lee and Jean, and they talked about how writers would

937
00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:23.850
write Michael in different ways.

938
00:40:23.910 --> 00:40:24.230
Yes.

939
00:40:24.530 --> 00:40:27.410
And I just think that's so smart of Phyllis to have observed that.

940
00:40:27.590 --> 00:40:27.770
Yeah.

941
00:40:28.250 --> 00:40:30.150
Phyllis went on to say, quote,

942
00:40:30.150 --> 00:40:35.490
I was new in that realm of the business, so I went out and bought books on improv because

943
00:40:35.490 --> 00:40:37.770
I had never really done improv before.

944
00:40:37.910 --> 00:40:42.590
And I knew that there had to be parameters and guidelines to go by, so I bought books to

945
00:40:42.590 --> 00:40:44.590
give me a little background on doing improv.

946
00:40:44.590 --> 00:40:48.350
But as far as my backstory, no, I did not create one.

947
00:40:49.030 --> 00:40:49.170
Yeah.

948
00:40:49.170 --> 00:40:53.570
But I mean, what she did was just powerful in its own way because she just wanted to be

949
00:40:53.570 --> 00:40:54.090
authentic.

950
00:40:55.270 --> 00:41:00.410
Well, speaking of backstories, we got a fan mail flurry from people wanting to know more

951
00:41:00.410 --> 00:41:04.630
about her character's romance with Bob Vance, Vance Refrigeration.

952
00:41:05.050 --> 00:41:06.770
Fan question from Michael C.

953
00:41:06.930 --> 00:41:07.830
in Staten Island.

954
00:41:08.230 --> 00:41:11.350
How does Phyllis think her character met Bob Vance?

955
00:41:11.610 --> 00:41:14.090
I would have loved to see the courtship.

956
00:41:14.530 --> 00:41:15.230
Me too.

957
00:41:16.070 --> 00:41:19.890
Well, Phyllis sent us in a little clip about this, and here's what she said.

958
00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:28.650
In my own mind, I always thought it would be humorous if I helped Bob change the tire on

959
00:41:28.650 --> 00:41:29.350
his car.

960
00:41:30.790 --> 00:41:37.390
You know, here is this really capable, strapping, strong man, and he needed help getting his

961
00:41:37.390 --> 00:41:42.450
flat tire on his car changed in the parking lot at Dunder Mifflin.

962
00:41:42.450 --> 00:41:45.570
And so I always thought it would be funny.

963
00:41:45.790 --> 00:41:50.970
So in my own mind, I helped Bob change the flat tire on his car.

964
00:41:51.690 --> 00:41:58.730
And as far as our romance, I always imagined that when Bob asked me to marry him, he rented

965
00:41:58.730 --> 00:42:00.950
out the booze cruise just for us.

966
00:42:01.070 --> 00:42:02.190
No one else was on it.

967
00:42:02.390 --> 00:42:08.930
He took me on the booze cruise, and it was just Bob Vance, Vance Refrigeration, and myself.

968
00:42:08.930 --> 00:42:11.650
And it was very romantic and really lovely.

969
00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:18.690
I'm not sure of the details, but you should ask Bob what his backstory was for us.

970
00:42:18.890 --> 00:42:20.710
He's really good at backstory.

971
00:42:20.950 --> 00:42:28.730
And I believe I read somewhere that he thought of Phyllis and Bob as the girl that he cared

972
00:42:28.730 --> 00:42:30.370
so much for in high school.

973
00:42:31.370 --> 00:42:36.910
Evidently, there was someone in his life that he really cared for, and he treated me as if

974
00:42:36.910 --> 00:42:40.610
I was that person that he cared so much for in high school.

975
00:42:41.830 --> 00:42:48.270
Also, his mother's name was Phyllis in real life, and his father's name was Bob.

976
00:42:48.510 --> 00:42:50.650
So that was kind of an interesting thing.

977
00:42:50.650 --> 00:42:55.930
And I must say that Bob was and is such a professional.

978
00:42:56.150 --> 00:43:03.630
He was always so respectful, and I really enjoyed our relationship of Phyllis and Bob Vance.

979
00:43:03.870 --> 00:43:08.950
He was wonderful to work with, and yeah, it was just pretty cool.

980
00:43:09.210 --> 00:43:11.810
So that's Bob and Phyllis.

981
00:43:12.650 --> 00:43:16.750
You know, I feel like you saw that.

982
00:43:16.970 --> 00:43:21.070
You witnessed their mutual respect and affection for one another.

983
00:43:21.330 --> 00:43:21.570
Yeah.

984
00:43:21.810 --> 00:43:26.630
I love her proposal story, that he rented out the booze cruise.

985
00:43:26.830 --> 00:43:27.050
Yeah.

986
00:43:28.030 --> 00:43:33.930
Gosh, they did such a good job of chemistry casting on our show.

987
00:43:34.350 --> 00:43:37.430
Yeah, and some of this just came about.

988
00:43:37.430 --> 00:43:41.370
You know, it's not like Phyllis and Bob had ever auditioned together.

989
00:43:41.610 --> 00:43:42.450
It's true.

990
00:43:43.070 --> 00:43:43.630
Yeah.

991
00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:48.870
I mean, he came on as Bob Vance, Vance Refrigeration, before we ever knew that he

992
00:43:48.870 --> 00:43:50.490
was going to be paired up with Phyllis.

993
00:43:50.630 --> 00:43:50.830
Yeah.

994
00:43:51.210 --> 00:43:51.490
Wow.

995
00:43:51.710 --> 00:43:56.050
And how crazy is it that his parents were named Bob and Phyllis?

996
00:43:56.210 --> 00:43:57.050
I know.

997
00:43:57.210 --> 00:43:58.410
What are the chances?

998
00:43:59.390 --> 00:44:02.510
Well, we also got another letter from Megan S.

999
00:44:02.710 --> 00:44:05.070
in San Diego, California, who said,

1000
00:44:05.070 --> 00:44:09.150
Why did Phyllis stay working after marrying Bob?

1001
00:44:09.630 --> 00:44:12.830
It was established he was a well-off business owner.

1002
00:44:13.550 --> 00:44:19.050
I know show-wise we needed her to stay, but I'm curious what Phyllis' reason was for not

1003
00:44:19.050 --> 00:44:21.270
retiring because of Bob's wealth.

1004
00:44:21.910 --> 00:44:24.030
Well, Megan, here's what Phyllis had to say.

1005
00:44:24.350 --> 00:44:27.630
Phyllis said, Well, Phyllis enjoyed her job.

1006
00:44:27.770 --> 00:44:32.850
She enjoyed the challenge of being a paper salesman, and she liked the company and the people and

1007
00:44:32.850 --> 00:44:34.690
the friends that she had at Dunder Mifflin.

1008
00:44:34.830 --> 00:44:37.130
And also, she didn't want to be fully dependent

1009
00:44:37.130 --> 00:44:37.890
upon a man.

1010
00:44:38.110 --> 00:44:40.430
She wanted to have her own source of income.

1011
00:44:41.630 --> 00:44:42.610
I believe it.

1012
00:44:42.730 --> 00:44:43.230
I do, too.

1013
00:44:43.650 --> 00:44:48.590
Also, you know, they met later in life, and she was a fully realized person.

1014
00:44:49.050 --> 00:44:50.910
You know, she didn't need Bob.

1015
00:44:51.070 --> 00:44:51.870
She chose Bob.

1016
00:44:52.850 --> 00:44:53.710
Oh, yeah.

1017
00:44:54.030 --> 00:44:54.330
Yeah.

1018
00:44:54.650 --> 00:44:55.130
Like that.

1019
00:44:55.130 --> 00:44:56.190
All right.

1020
00:44:56.270 --> 00:44:59.110
So, you know how earlier we all shared our favorite Phyllis moments?

1021
00:44:59.330 --> 00:44:59.570
Yes.

1022
00:44:59.850 --> 00:45:04.030
Well, we got this fan question from Sion J in London, England.

1023
00:45:04.650 --> 00:45:05.650
Sion says,

1024
00:45:05.890 --> 00:45:09.710
Hey, Phyllis, you've got that perfect mix of sweet and sassy.

1025
00:45:09.970 --> 00:45:11.490
What's your favorite Phyllis

